robin <usenet@unrated.net> wrote:

>The simple fact is that NetHack is what the Dev Team does with it; it's 
>_their_ program, not ours.


The relevance of "ownership" here never fails to boggle me.  The code
and the name were inherited from still other people in the past, and
there's no profit involved.  The very name net-hack underlines its
cooperative heritage.  It's a project perfectly suited for shared,
active development with its enthusiastic fan base, not keeping away
from others in the name of property rights.


>As many folks have said, if that makes you 
>unhappy you are welcome to start your own development effort....


Same refrain - branching and competition have not been the most
productive avenues for the players, I sincerely believe.


>It's _very_ clear at this point that the Dev Team is not going to change 
>their style to suit you....


Then perhaps they'll become irrelevant, if enough people tire of
waiting on them while they exercise their rights to the game.


>The 
>/dev/null/nethack tournament would not exist at all if we'd spent our 
>time trying to get the Dev Team to hold one rather than taking what they 
>had produced and building something new out of it ourselves;  NAO 
>wouldn't exist if they'd spent their time trying to get the Dev Team to 
>run a game hosting system rather than building one themselves.


Not good analogies.  I didn't suggest they take on new, ancillary
projects, but rather share or cede development of the game to
interested and more energetic parties, in a participatory environment.


>The Dev Team is what it is, and they work the way they choose; if you 
>want something else, you really are going to have to take responsibility 
>for building it.


Then the best gift they can give on the way out is ceding control of
the name, website, and "ownership", so others are not duplicating
effort and working at cross-purposes with them.

